// Distributed schedule for example 08-histogram — STRETCH.
//
// AC#3 of TASK-0044.04 carries this schedule: 'distributed.sched.nuc
// lowers + emits + runs bit-identical on at least one tier-1
// backend that supports partition-then-reduce'. The pthreads-sync
// path through TASK-0258 (partition_rows / partition_workers) is
// the most-established route.
//
// The shape this aims at:
//   host    — owns I/O (load_input / save_output).
//   w0..w3  — each compute worker owns one partition of `input`
//             and produces a partial bin-increment fold over its
//             slice. The compiler partitions the outer `i` loop
//             across the four workers.
//
// Cross-worker edges this schedule declares:
//   input      host -> { w0..w3 }   (host loads, workers consume)
//   histogram  { w0..w3 } -> host   (workers produce, host
//                                    receives the reduced output)
//
// All `sync` (the pthreads-sync backend's only transfer mode).
// `partition=workers` is the partition policy for the outer `i`
// loop — the same shape examples 03-reduction and 04-prefix-sum
// use.
//
// Honest open question (filed cycle 186 if the cell lowers but
// emits wrong output): the cross-worker partial-histogram combine
// is a substantively new ACFG shape vs the SCALAR-combine of
// 03-reduction. 03-reduction reduces N i32 values into ONE i32 via
// pairwise `combine(a, b)`; here the per-worker partial is a
// BINS-element array that must be element-wise summed across all
// four workers into the host's `histogram`. The current ACFG
// machinery for the `histogram[b] <-- bin_inc(...)` statement
// expresses this as a per-worker rectangular fold; whether the
// cross-worker reduce phase lowers depends on whether the
// compiler treats the LHS-indexed accumulator as a partition-
// reducible shape. If the cell rejects with UnsupportedFeature,
// that surface is the new gap to file.

schedule for "../prog.algo.nuc" {
    workers = { host, w0, w1, w2, w3 };

    // I/O on host.
    place load_input  on host;
    place save_output on host;

    // The pure step kernel distributed across compute workers.
    place bin_inc on { w0, w1, w2, w3 };

    // Outer loop is the partition axis; one slice per compute worker.
    loop i : partition=workers;

    // Cross-worker data — one transfer per data symbol that crosses.
    transfer input     : sync;
    transfer histogram : sync;
}
